From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 TCON_TV1 -- the timing generator, and its own video source

[Recipe cut: the debugfs files enable, source, pattern and scanout are not
carried; the raster is started by the KMS master only.]

The block that turns a pixel clock into a raster, and the second half of the
pixel path this tree can now drive from a driver rather than by hand. TCON_TV1
sits at 0x05731000, feeds the DisplayPort transmitter at 0x05740000, and can be
its own video source -- which is why a picture on this SoC needs neither a
display engine nor a KMS driver.

THIS IS A TRANSCRIPTION OF A PROVEN SEQUENCE
--------------------------------------------

Fifteen registers, driven by hand on the 8 GiB board on 2026-08-18 with the effect
watched at each step, and the result was a 1920x1200 test pattern on a
DisplayPort monitor, confirmed by eye. The target state was cross-read the
same day off a live vendor link: fifteen non-zero words in the whole 4 KiB
window, which is the most useful number in that capture:
whatever a TCON driver for this SoC is, it is not large.

Every field comes from the A733 UM's own TCON_TV register list, chapter
15.2.10, not from the classic sunxi TCON1 layout -- that would be the wrong
provenance, and it is why this file names the four words the UM does NOT
describe individually instead of guessing at them.

WHAT IS COMPUTED, WHAT IS CARRIED, AND WHAT IS DROPPED
------------------------------------------------------

| register              | this driver                                          |
|-----------------------|------------------------------------------------------|
| TV_BASIC1..5          | computed from the mode: the mode minus one throughout |
|                       | with the vertical total doubled, as 15.2.10.7 says    |
| TV_CTL START_DELAY    | computed, blanking lines minus five -- see below      |
| TV_IO_POL             | two bits from the mode's sync flags, IO2_INV carried   |
| TV_PIXELDEPTH_MODE    | computed from bpc; must agree with the DP side's MISC0|
| TV_SRC_CTL            | VDBIST + pattern, both reachable from debugfs         |
| TV_GCTL, TV_IO_TRI    | carried; TV_GCTL bit 31 is not in the UM              |
| TV_GINT0              | ZERO, not the vendor's interrupt enable -- see below   |
| TV_DEBUG              | ZERO, which is the CLEAR of its two W0C latches       |
| TCON 0x0500, 0x0FE8   | carried, in no UM register list at all                |
| TCON 0x0304, 0x0308   | NOT written -- settled, see below                     |
| VO1 0x0000, 0x0020    | computed: the two documented VIDEO_OUT1_SYS registers |
| VO1 0x00F4            | carried, in no UM register list                       |

THREE OF THOSE ARE DEPARTURES FROM THE BY-HAND RUN, EACH NAMED IN THE CODE

1. TV_GINT0_REG is written zero where the run writes TV_VB_INT_EN. Nothing in
   this tree services a TCON interrupt, and an enabled interrupt with no handler
   is a latch nobody clears. Falsified by: the scan stopping without it.

2. TV_DEBUG_REG is written zero where the run writes back the 0x000002E5 it read
   from a live link. Only bits 30 and 29 of that register are writable, both are
   R/W0C, and [15:0] is a read-only line counter -- so zero is the clear, and
   0x2E5 is a line number written into a counter.

3. TV_FILL_BEGIN (0x0304) and TV_FILL_END (0x0308) are not written, and this one
   is settled rather than argued. Both are documented, at 0x304 + N*0x0C and
   0x308 + N*0x0C (UM 15.2.10.18/19), which is why a plain offset search misses
   them; both are gated by TV_FILL_CTL_REG bit 31 TV_FILL_EN, "0: Bypass", which
   is zero at reset, zero on the vendor's live link and zero in our own run. And
   the two values are explained: the vendor programs FILL_BEGIN = (vtotal+1)<<12
   and FILL_END = (vtotal+vactive_space)<<12 every modeset
   (tcon_tv_reg.c:147-153), so 0x004D4000 / 0x004D3000 are 1236<<12 and 1235<<12
   against VTOTAL 1235 -- its own arithmetic with the feature switched off.

THE ONE UNDOCUMENTED WORD THAT IS LOAD-BEARING
-----------------------------------------------

TV_GCTL_REG bit 31. UM 15.2.10.1 gives this register [5:4] PIXEL_MODE, bit 0
UNSTRICT_TIMING_MODE_SEL and calls [31:6] reserved, yet both the vendor's live
link and the run that produced a picture carry 0x80000000 and nothing scans
without it. Every sunxi TCON has called the same bit TCON_EN; that is a
hypothesis about why it works, not a source, and the comment on
TV_GCTL_REG in the code says so.

⬜ TCON 0x0500, TCON 0x0FE8 and VO1 0x00F4 are in no UM list, and there is now a
cheap experiment for all three: the VENDOR'S OWN DRIVER DOES NOT WRITE THEM
EITHER. Its TCON_TV register model stops at 0x340 (tcon_tv_reg.h:415) and its
TCON-TOP model at 0x2C (tcon_top_type.h:216-231). So what a live vendor link
carries at those offsets is power-on state or the bootloader's, which makes the
three writes candidates for deletion. Reading them at the U-Boot prompt settles
it, exactly as it settled PLL_VIDEO1 bit 24 on 2026-08-19.

START_DELAY -- A HINT THAT VERIFIED, AND TWO DEFECTS NOT TAKEN
--------------------------------------------------------------

UM 15.2.10.6 gives the field ([19:4]) and its meaning (it delays the release of
the SYNC signal towards the DE, in lines; with a value of 2, de_syn stays valid
until the end of the 2nd line) and no value. The vendor computes
vertical blanking minus five and a live link carries exactly that: 1235 - 1200 -
5 = 30 = the 0x1E in 0x800001E0. The formula is taken because it reproduces
silicon; the five is a bare magic number in that code and is named as one here
rather than presented as a derivation.

Two defects in that code are not taken, and both are silent:

  - it declares the field FIVE bits wide, start_delay:5 at [8:4]
    (tcon_tv_reg.h:66), against the UM's [19:4]. It therefore clamps at 31 and
    pins every mode with more than 36 blanking lines to the same delay.
  - the subtraction runs on a u32 (tcon_tv_reg.c:154-157), so a mode with fewer
    than five blanking lines wraps to ~4.29e9, fails the unsigned "> 31" test
    and is programmed as the MAXIMUM delay -- for the mode that wanted the
    minimum.

VDBIST IS A MODE, NOT THE ARCHITECTURE
---------------------------------------

TV_SRC_CTL_REG bit 0 chooses the data source: 0 takes the picture from the
display engine, 1 from the VDBIST pattern generator, and [2:1]
picks colour bars, greyscale, black-by-white or gridding. UM 15.2.6.3 describes
the generator as a test aid for exercising and debugging the lane path from
TCON_TV through HDMI/eDP to the screen.

That bit is why a picture needs no display engine -- and it is also why a
picture can never be a console. So it is one register bit here, reachable both
ways from debugfs in the register's own 0/1 encoding, and every other register
in this driver is derived from a struct drm_display_mode. Writing 0 to `source`
today gives a black screen and that is the correct answer, not a bug: there is
no display-engine driver yet to be the source. When there is, it clears that bit
and nothing in this file changes shape.

THE OBSERVABLE THIS PATCH OWES
-------------------------------

Not "the registers read back". TV_DEBUG_REG [15:0] TV_CURRENT_LINE is a counter
the TCON's own scan drives, bounded by the programmed VTOTAL. debugfs `scanout`
samples it 512 times ~100 us apart -- spread deliberately, because 512 reads
back to back span microseconds and would report a stuck counter on a perfectly
scanning TCON -- and reports min, max, wraps and a verdict against the mode's
VTOTAL with a tolerance derived from the sampling stride, not picked.

Next to it, the two sticky data-supply latches, read before and after the
window so an old underflow is not reported as a new one. Nothing has ever
exercised them here. ⬜ The UM and the vendor disagree about bit 29: the UM
calls it LOSTWARN, the vendor's model calls it TCON_TV0's field
polarity (tcon_tv_reg.h:185-198). They agree on bit 30, which is this TCON's
FIFO underflow either way, and TCON_TV0 is dark on this board -- so the report
is safe under both readings and the ambiguity is recorded rather than resolved.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS HERE, AND WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------------------------------------------------

Observations from the vendor BSP source
(bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_device/{sunxi_tcon.c,hardware/lowlevel_tcon/*}).

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| `tcon_tv_close()` disables a different interrupt than open enables: open enables LCD_IRQ_TCON1_VBLK (14), close clears LCD_IRQ_TCON0_VBLK (15) -- tcon_tv_reg.c:101 against :109, so bit 14 stays set. The TCON_TV vblank interrupt is never disabled | no interrupt is enabled at all, because nothing here services one, and the reason is recorded here |
| the IRQ is freed BEFORE the source is stopped (sunxi_tcon.c:834 before :835-836), and `sunxi_tcon_free_irq()` can decline to free at all, comparing a pointer against a struct that every mode_init overwrites by memcpy -- the line then leaks and the next request on a non-shared IRQ returns -EBUSY | — |
| FIFO underflow is sampled every vblank and dropped: tcon_tv_reg.c:91 clears it, sunxi_drm_crtc.c:1595 increments a counter, and that counter is printed only in a debugfs atomic-state dump (:1830). No log line, no recovery | read and reported with the line counter, before and after a measured window, with the UM's own words for what each latch means |
| TV_FILL_BEGIN/END/DATA written on every modeset into a block whose enable is gated on `vactive_space`, which no code in that tree ever assigns -- so FILL_END always lands below FILL_BEGIN and FILL_EN is always 0 | not written; the gate is read from the UM and the values are explained rather than carried |
| START_DELAY truncated to five bits and computed with an unsigned subtraction that wraps | the UM's field width, and a guarded subtraction |
| `is_enabled = true` is set before the bring-up begins (sunxi_tcon.c:799) and not one of the five calls after it -- clocks, resets, rate, timings, IRQ -- has its return value checked; `sunxi_tcon_edp_mode_init` returns 0 unconditionally, and its caller ignores that too | every clock, reset and rate call checked and unwound; `enabled` set only after the last of them |
| `devm_ioremap_resource()` tested against NULL (sunxi_tcon.c:890, sunxi_tcon_top.c:63) -- it returns an ERR_PTR, so the check is dead and an error pointer is dereferenced as MMIO. `platform_get_irq` tested with `if (!irq_no)` (:896), likewise dead for a negative errno | IS_ERR / dev_err_probe throughout |
| the pixel clock is set with `clk_set_rate()` and the result never read back (sunxi_tcon.c:801-802) | asked for, then read back and refused if the clock model did not deliver it -- PLL_VIDEO1 and EDP_TV_CLK are new in this tree and are the thing under test |
| its `colorbar` sysfs writes a 3-bit `src_sel` starting at bit 0 (tcon_tv_reg.h:56), which straddles the UM's VDBIST_MODE (bit 0) and TV_SRC_SEL ([2:1]) -- so half of its 0..7 range silently turns the pattern generator OFF | the two fields are separate, named, and carry the UM's own encoding |
| ~200 dead lines in one 579-line file: `tcon_tv_ceu()` with a 91-entry sine table and five colour matrices, zero callers, and external linkage, so it stays in the build; `tcon_de_attach()` whose entire 30-line body sits after an unconditional `return 0` | — |
| no `readl`/`writel` anywhere in the TCON layer: a `volatile struct *` with bitfield stores, `__iomem` cast through `uintptr_t` (sunxi_tcon.c:888), and therefore no barriers between register writes and the clock/reset calls around them | normal MMIO accessors |

AND WHAT IT GETS RIGHT, WHICH IS TAKEN RATHER THAN DIFFERENTIATED FROM

  - The enable ORDER. It clears TV_EN, the module enable and both halves of the
    interrupt register before touching a timing register, and asserts TV_EN last
    (tcon_tv_reg.c:29-38, :100). Off a fresh reset those first writes are no-ops,
    which is why the by-hand run never needed them -- they are what makes a
    second enable, or an enable over a bootloader's splash, land in a defined
    state. This driver does the same and says where it comes from.
  - Sync polarity is derived from the mode, not hardcoded, and the SAME two
    flags are fed to the DisplayPort side's MSA polarity register -- so the TCON
    and the transmitter cannot describe different modes. That is the right
    architecture and this driver keeps it, with the mode as the single source.
  - Timing arithmetic is consistently zero-based and folds the sync pulse into
    the back porch, which is what the registers want.

THE DEVICE TREE THIS CONSUMES, AND THE TWO THINGS IT DOES NOT FIND THERE
------------------------------------------------------------------------

The node is added by `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` on the branch that models the pixel clock, so this
patch contains no DTS and takes what that node describes:

    tcon_tv1: lcd-controller@5731000 {
            compatible = "allwinner,sun60i-a733-tcon-tv";
            reg = <0x0 0x05731000 0x0 0x1000>;
            clocks = <&ccu CLK_BUS_TCON_TV1>, <&ccu CLK_EDP_TV>;
            clock-names = "bus", "tcon-tv";
            resets = <&ccu RST_BUS_TCON_TV1>;
    };

⬜ TWO THINGS THE HARDWARE NEEDS AND THAT NODE DOES NOT CARRY. Both are
optional in this driver and neither is optional on silicon, so both are named
at enable with their addresses rather than skipped silently:

  1. VIDEO_OUT1_SYS (DISPLAY1_TOP) at 0x05510000. Two registers out of its
     4 KiB: TV_CLK_EDP_I2S1_SRC bit 5 and VO1_MODULE_GATING bit 21 TV1_GATE.
     Without them this TCON's clock comes from the HDMI side and its output
     stays gated -- and the symptom is a perfect-looking TCON register set and
     no picture. `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` states the intent to give that block a node of its own,
     the shape mainline uses for sun8i-tcon-top, and that the node is owed;
     until it exists this driver takes an OPTIONAL second reg window, and
     without either it logs:

       no VIDEO_OUT1_SYS window: 0x05510000 bit 5 (clock from EDP) and
       0x05510020 bit 21 (TV1_GATE) are NOT set

  2. The DPSS_TOP1 gate and reset (DPSS_TOP1_BGR, CCU 0x16CC). TOP1 is in the
     path and TOP0 is not. Taken as an optional "dpss" clock and reset here;
     absent, enable logs that 0x020036CC is not touched.

Whether those live on this node or on a block of their own is the device
tree's decision, not this driver's -- which is why they are optional lookups
and a loud warning rather than a probe failure. A bring-up session can make
both by hand; a KMS driver must not inherit a silent gap.

VO1_MODULE_GATING was first listed here as one of the words absent from the UM.
It is not: UM 15.2.9.2 names bits 28, 21 and 20, and the 0x00200000 a live
link carries is exactly TV1_GATE released with TV0's two gates shut.

The node's `interrupts = <GIC_SPI 58 ...>` is deliberately not requested: this
driver enables no TCON interrupt, for the reason given above.

FILES
-----

This patch adds
drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-tcon-tv.c and sun60i-display.h, and edits
drivers/gpu/drm/Makefile plus the Kconfig and Makefile that
`drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` created.
The device-tree half is not in this patch.

drivers/gpu/drm/Makefile's `obj-$(CONFIG_DRM_SUN60I_DP) += sun60i/` becomes
`obj-y += sun60i/` for the same reason `obj-y += renesas/` reads that way: the
directory now holds two independent tristates, and the old line would skip the
whole directory when only the TCON is enabled.

WHAT IT OWES
------------

  1. THE SCAN. With a sink on J4, the port in a DP pin assignment and the CCU
     patches present. ⚠ The debugfs directory is named after the DT node, and
     `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window` calls it lcd-controller@5731000, so it is 5731000.lcd-controller and
     not 5731000.tcon:

       echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/enable
       cat     /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/scanout

     PASS is the verdict line reading SCANNING with a maximum of 1235 -- the
     programmed VTOTAL -- and at least one wrap over the 512 samples. Falsified
     by: a stuck counter (nothing is scanning), a maximum above 1235 (the timing
     went in wrong), or `underflow`/`lostwarn` set during the window.
  2. THE REGISTER STATE, against the register values of a working link:
     0x05731090 = 0x800001E0, 0x0573109C = 0x077F04AF, 0x057310A0 = 0x081F006F,
     0x057310A4 = 0x09A6001F, 0x057310A8 = 0x001F0005, 0x05731098 = 0x000009A6,
     0x05731088 = 0x06000000, 0x0573108C = 0x0FFFFFFF, 0x05731040 = 0x00000001,
     0x05731340 = 0x00000001, 0x05731000 = 0x80000000, 0x05510000 = 0x00000020,
     0x05510020 = 0x00200000. Two differences from those values are intended and are
     the point: 0x05731004 reads 0x00000000 rather than 0x40000000, and 0x05731304
     / 0x05731308 read 0x00000000 rather than 0x004D4000 / 0x004D3000.
  3. A PICTURE, by eye, with the transmitter's half (`drm-a733-dp-the-main-video-stream`) and link training.
     Nothing else proves the pattern is on the screen -- a register
     read-back is not that proof.

Build-tested only -- not hardware-proven. Applies at fuzz 0 on the full series; the
sun60i/ directory builds W=1 warning-free with CONFIG_DRM_SUN60I_TCON_TV=m;
checkpatch --strict reports 0 errors, 0 warnings, 0 checks.

Assisted-by: Claude

